   "id": "OS-01",
   "severity": "medium",
   "title": "Official week pages render completed games as 'Hidden Score'",
   "detail": "https://www.nfl.com/schedules/2026/by-week/week-9 draws every tile with the literal string 'Hidden Score' in the score slot, but the tile headline above it carries the final score as text ('Packers 45, Patriots 27, FINAL'). Week 1 and Week 10 behave the same way. The headline text was used; the tile text was treated as absent. Any automation that reads the tile body rather than the headline will record a blank or, worse, the words 'Hidden Score'.",
   "impact": "Reading the official page programmatically is unreliable for scores. The Game Book PDF served from static.www.nfl.com is reliable and is used as the cross-check for every graded row in this namespace.",
   "sources": [
    "https://www.nfl.com/schedules/2026/by-week/week-9",
    "https://www.nfl.com/schedules/2026/by-week/week-1"
   ],
   "status": "needs review"
  },
  {
   "id": "OS-02",
   "severity": "high",
   "title": "LA @ WAS (Week 9) is unreadable on the game page itself",
   "detail": "https://www.nfl.com/games/rams-at-commanders-2026-reg-9 renders 'Hidden Score' for both teams in the scoreline, in every quarter cell and in the 'Previous Matchup' card. The Game Book for the same game (ab382331-4feb-11f1-abca-2c54536568a9.pdf) shows the full box score: LA 20, WAS 13, final. The score is recorded in this namespace from the Game Book, and the game-page ambiguity is published here rather than silently smoothed over.",
   "impact": "One Week 9 result can only be sourced from the Game Book PDF, not from the game page. The Game Book is the NFL's own official document, so the row is graded; the anomaly stays visible.",
   "sources": [
    "https://www.nfl.com/games/rams-at-commanders-2026-reg-9",
    "https://static.www.nfl.com/image/upload/v1794224919/gamecenter/ab382331-4feb-11f1-abca-2c54536568a9.pdf"
   ],
   "status": "needs review"
  }
